                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "51050520-dbd1-3be2-994c-a2438982f4a8",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A place to urinate and defecate: an outhouse or lavatory."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Australia",
                                            "UK",
                                            "slang"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1475a8ea-f4c8-4ea5-8a39-6e61583fbb18",
                                                "sense_id": "51050520-dbd1-3be2-994c-a2438982f4a8",
                                                "text": "In Cubbaroo's dim distant past\n They built a double dyke.\n Back to back in the yard it stood\n An architectural dream in wood."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3443eb30-088f-3cbb-a256-ab1b62b201c1",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An embankment formed by the spoil from the creation of a ditch."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "17008c8f-4888-35a5-8100-f94c073a3262",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A wall, especially (obsolete outside heraldry) a masoned city or castle wall."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ee691d47-14ea-4749-b336-f3b9394012fc",
                                                "sense_id": "17008c8f-4888-35a5-8100-f94c073a3262",
                                                "text": "Inchbrakie gives for Arms, Or, a Dyke (or Wall) Feſsways, broke down in ſome places, and in Baſe a Roſe Gules, on a chief Sable three Eſcalops of the firſt. The Dyke (or Wall) here, is aſſumed not only to difference, but to perpetuate the valiant Action of Graham before mentioned; in throwing down the Wall and Ditch, which the Romans made betwixt Forth and Clyde, to keep out the Scots, [...]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8c61334b-51b4-46f0-8774-9f915eb7e959",
                                                "sense_id": "17008c8f-4888-35a5-8100-f94c073a3262",
                                                "text": "Wall, (sometimes called a dyke, fr. mur) : this is generally found in connection with castles or towns which are walled (muraillé). A wall of which kind should be masoned (fr. maçonné) and embattled (fr. crenellé), even though this be not specified. [...] Or, a dyke [or wall] fesswise [masoned proper] broken down in some places gules; [...] —Graham, Inchbrakie, Scotland [similar arms borne by Græme of Stapleton]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "416476e1-e060-486f-a31b-d721bba5b9ad",
                                                "sense_id": "17008c8f-4888-35a5-8100-f94c073a3262",
                                                "text": "The Galloway Dyke / In southwest Scotland there is a local style of dry stone dyke that is now recognized as 'the Galloway dyke', although when this pattern of was first developed, it was simply described as 'the sheep dyke'."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c97f2985-ee89-3be2-b7a3-000294a948c6",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A low embankment or stone wall serving as an enclosure and boundary marker."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Scotland",
                                            "UK"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b1e60a26-b91c-4b2a-80ae-ed4354549603",
                                                "sense_id": "c97f2985-ee89-3be2-b7a3-000294a948c6",
                                                "text": "The Galloway Dyke / In southwest Scotland there is a local style of dry stone dyke that is now recognized as 'the Galloway dyke', although when this pattern of was first developed, it was simply described as 'the sheep dyke'."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c81c7a16-b593-3976-b828-45bdcff159d7",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any fence or hedge."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK",
                                            "dialectal"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "513125c9-b084-3192-ab20-7d736175254f",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An earthwork raised to prevent inundation of low land by the sea or flooding rivers."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "643470f1-c433-4bf3-8cdf-25afb6d6ece2",
                                                "sense_id": "513125c9-b084-3192-ab20-7d736175254f",
                                                "text": "The king of Texcuco advised the building of a great dike, so thick and strong as to keep out the water."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "60bff63d-cd38-4fd7-9472-714bd0965310",
                                                "sense_id": "513125c9-b084-3192-ab20-7d736175254f",
                                                "text": "Serious as was the flood damage in England, the bursting of some of the dykes on the coast of Holland resulted in an even more widespread and devastating inundation."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d46095c5-f2ee-31bb-8e14-df2442cce9e8",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any impediment, barrier, or difficulty."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK",
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e4c6ee37-24ff-357e-ab76-e3d8e9af67c9",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A beaver's dam."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c83414ec-8c63-3afe-8b90-0b4974a799fd",
                                        "entry_id": "23d7a040-54f6-34b5-b3f6-301f9de30b97",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A jetty; a pier."
